Hi,

I noticed that for fp->int conversions on ev5, the Compaq C compiler
generates code like

  30:   e0 05 e0 5b     cvttq/c $f0,$f0
  34:   00 00 1e 9c     stt     $f0,0(sp)
  38:   9e 04 fe 47     cmoveq  zero,sp,sp
  3c:   00 00 fe 2f     unop
  40:   00 00 1e a4     ldq     v0,0(sp)

or

  30:   00 00 1c 88     lds     $f0,0(at)
  34:   00 14 00 5a     addt    $f16,$f0,$f0
  38:   e0 05 e0 5b     cvttq/c $f0,$f0
  3c:   00 00 1e 9c     stt     $f0,0(sp)
  40:   1e 08 df 47     xor     sp,zero,sp
  44:   00 00 1e a4     ldq     v0,0(sp)

Now what is this fiddling with the stack pointer doing? Does it avoid
the replay trap? The HRM says: "If the load instruction is scheduled
to issue two cycles after the store instruction, then it will be
issue-stalled for one cycle." Does the xor avoid this cycle?

-- 
