>   Bing!  Two major gotchas right there.  First, either figure out how
> much cache you have, or jumper the board to the _least_ possible amount.
> Second, I can tell you, from personal experience with 3 different 164LX
> boards, you absolutely need ECC registered PC100 RAM.  No real alternative, 
> if you want a stable system.  IMHO, if it's a production
> system, you should bite the bullet and buy RAM specifically for that
> board.  I got Kingston 128M DIMMs a few months ago for less than
> $.50/MB.  All my troubles went away....  And all your troubles, except 
> 
>   FYI, the 164LX is a "half-flash" BIOS - you can have either AlphaBIOS or SRM, 
> but not both.  IIRC, typing "fwupdate" at the SRM prompt will reboot off the 
> floppy.  Also IIRC, the file on the floppy must be named lx164_v5_8.exe to 
> actually update the BIOS.  If it won't boot from the lx164nt.rom floppy, try 
> following the instructions at (watch the line wrap):
> http://www.support.compaq.com/alpha-tools/documentation/current/pc164lx/
> 	ec-r2zpc-te_164lx_unix_users_man.pdf
> 
>   Section 7.4.2 explains how to update the BIOS.  If you're going to run Linux 
> or Unix on it, going to v5.8 is worth the trouble.  Installing VMS on that 
> board requires an Act of Congreaa _and_ a Presidential Directive.
> 
>   There's a v5.8 firmware floppy image at:
> http://samsonfile.samson.cs.sunysb.edu/~samson/Alpha/ALPHA_BIOS_568.floppy
> 
>   dd that (or rawrite in DOS mode on a Windows box) to a 1.44 floppy and you 
> should be good to go.
> 
> http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/Alpha/firmware/archive/alphapc164lx/
>   has all the actual update stuff.  Gatekeeper isn't connecting right now.  
> 
>   Good luck.
