> Thanks for the help, yes my power supplies have a hard power on off
> switch - so I should be fine.  I didn't know how safe shorting the
> power-good pin on the connector was.  I'm experienced wrt software but
> just now getting more into the hands on hardware aspect.  At the risk
> of being told to RTFM, how do I go about shorting the needed pin?
> 

Thanks to Google, this is pretty easy... 'ATX pinout' immediately had a
nice page at top ranking: http://xtronics.com/reference/atx_pinout.htm.

You have to short the PS-ON pin with some GND pin. Lazy me just stuck a
piece of wire (solder wire!) from the top (where the cables from PS go
in) into the contacts. 

Hasn't the PS 500 case a distinguished Power switch at the front? If
that's a real switch and not just a button type, you could of course use
this one as a real front switch.

