On 21 Jun 2002 16:55:55 +0400, Wartan Hachaturow <wart@softhome.net> wrote:
> 
> Since only component market I've got an access to is 
> a PC market, I now wonder what kind of cards should I buy.
> Perhaps, you could give me some recommendations?
> It would also be nice, if the hardware would work fine 
> under both OSes ;)
> 
> 1. Video card.

  I've had good luck with #9 GXE64 in all my Alphas.  Not a lot of
colors, but good support and stability.
 
> 2. Hard drive.
> 
> I think I don't need a SCSI drive, since this would be a
> true home workstation. Will general large IDE drives available
> work?

  The IDE controller on the 164LX is slow as molasses, by PC standards.
If you can, you should use SCSI drives.  You'll like your LX much
better.  I like a card based on the NCR 53C875 chip, but IIRC, you need
a 53C810 adapter for VMS.
 
> 3. Network card.

  I haven't run Tru64 with anything but a true DEC405, but have run VMS 
and Linux on the 164LX with generic (Kingston) DEC2104x adapters.
 
> No idea here. Perhaps, rtl8139-based?

  Not to step on anyone's toes, but Realtek is as close to a Win-NIC as 
there is.  All the Realtek chipsets eat bus I/O.  Like a Winmodem, they 
use the main processor to handle operations that a good NIC does on-chip.
This not only takes up CPU time, but greatly increases I/O on the PCI bus.
  We've found that heavy traffic across 3 or 4 of the 8139s can swamp a 
pretty fast machine.

> 4. Sound card. 
> 
> Alpha linux people say Creative SB Live! works fine. Will it
> work under Tru64? (Not that I want to play games, but I like
> to listen to mp3 while working ;).

  SoundBlaster 32, Vibra, or 16 is my preference.  There was one PCI SB that
causes a kernel panic in FreeBSD. 

	Doc


