From memory,

the AlphaServer 2100 is one of the first Alpha's we made, let's see, I think
I remmeber letting the first one out the door in, yes, that''l do, I am very
old, well, mmeory fails me on the date, but it was shortly after the DECpc
AXP150, we let loose.

The Alpha Server was made in two distinct variations for slae as a server
and another two variations for Custom Systems.

First the floor units, were in long low cabinets, model 1 had 8 EISA slots
and x(mumble) PCI slots model 2 had 8 PCI slots and x(mumble) EISA slots.
Over the lifetime of the products various CPU and memory upgrades became
available and at one point, I remember a CPU backplane change required for
some CPU's.
The base system had a single Power Supply, but anything with 2 CPU's and
more than 2 memory boards had two Power Supplies.

The Custom Systems variations of the 2100 were Rack mount units, one being
almost identical to it's floor mate, just completely re=packaged with
different power supplies and the other being a very height reduced rack
mount unit.

They run OpenVMS, Tru64 UNIX, Windows NT V4.0 and LINUX from what I
remember.

I demo'd the first systems off the line to Corel, running Windows NT with
RAID Level 5 and they purchased a bunch for servers right away.

The RZ55 is a full height 5.25 inch disk, I believ it was 500MB or so. It's
a very good work horse. The TZ30 was one of the nicer implementations of the
Linear Tape drive family I liked, but it's capacity is only 90MB or so. It's
the same as a TK50 or TZK50, take you pick, it's SCSI.

rtt

If you want dollar values, $10.00, $1.00 and $0.25 respectively, unless, you
are a DEC used reseller, then they seem to get whatever they charge for the
stuff.



