Christian Mason wrote:
> 
> I've recently purchased a DEC 500a sans power supply.  The board
> appeared to take a standard 20-pin ATX connector.   I borrowed a 200W
> ATX pwr. supply from another unit and tried to power the beast up.....
> nothing.  The schematic for this board shows that it has two pins that
> should be connected to the "halt" button.  There are no wires coming
> from the on/off button on the front of the case to the mobo.

Same for other boards, like the OEM PC164. The soft start pins at the
ATX conncetor are simply not wired. Apparently, DEC used a special PS
that had it's own soft start circuit. 

>      I've been assured that this mobo. and system are good.  Is there
> some way I can trick a standard ATX supply into powering it up?   I.e.
> can I short the power good pin on the power supply while having it
> power the motherboard?

If you are lucky, your ATX pwr supply has a separate mains switch at the
rear, near the plugs for main and perhaps, the extender to the monitor.
Just short the soft start pin to gound, let's see...
If the locking nose is facing you, it the single-colored forth pin from
left (cables going upwards). Unfortunately, you need a real on-off
switch here, and not just a momentary one, as used in ATX PCs.

