If your box says storage expansion on the front, and VS42 something on 
the back, than the cover and the box don't belong together. A storage 
expansion box has type BA42 something. A VS42 is a VAX 3100 series 
system, I am not sure what yours exactly is.

Most older SCSI-2 drives work in these systems. You can't boot from a 
drive which is larger than 1 GB. Of course you want to run OpenVMS.

The graphics adapter is an other story. If there is an expansion board 
mounted on top of the motherboard, just under the disks mounting plate, 
than you have a color display. It can be either a GPX or a SPX version. 
The GPX has a resolution of 1024 x 860 x 8 bits; the SPX has 1280 x 1024 
x 256. Both have sync over green. The Digital VRT16 and VRT19 monitors 
were normally used with these systems.

If there is no expansion board, than you can use the monochrome display 
adapter on the mother board.

Any modern monitor may work, but you will have to build an adapter for 
the connector your self. I don't think you can buy them.

HTH,

Bart Zorn



