In article <Xns9214DC2EFC7EBjde59@204.118.133.26>, Jake <jde59_nospam@yahoo.com> writes:
:I have a question that I could use some help on.  Unfortunately I don't 
:have a lot of information about the systems I'm going to be dealing with. 

  You need to acquire the configuration information, lest we miss
  something during the ensuing speculation.

:I haven't used a VAX system 

  VAX is hardware, and generally quite useless without operating system 
  software installed and running.  One of the operating systems commonly 
  used on VAX hardware is OpenVMS.  Specifically, OpenVMS VAX.  OpenVMS 
  VAX V7.3 is the current release.

:...for some time now and am re-learning it at a slow pace.  Here is my 
:dilema.  Two simulators are in play.  While the application software is 
:essentially the same, the platforms are not. One is based on an Alpha 
:using UNIX (DECUnix I think) and the other is a VMS based system. 

  "Essentially" meaning exactly what, here?

:I currently don't  know if it is an Alpha or a VAX, I'm still trying to 
:get that information.  

  This would certainly be useful for us to know, but the particular 
  hardware is (usually) of less interest than the operating system 
  and (in this case) the version.  OpenVMS can provide network file
  transfers, on VAX, Alpha and (as the current port proceeds) IA-64
  platforms -- most commonly, OpenVMS with networking software.

:Basically I need to transfer a data file (Simulation Library) from the UNIX 
:based system to the VMS based system.  

  On OpenVMS V6.2 or later with an IP stack configured, use the
  following command:

  $ COPY/FTP/ANON/BIN filespec unix.ip.address::"/target/dir/name"

:Does anyone have any helpful suggestions or know of any software 
:applications that will allow me to port the file over? 

  "Port" implies more than a transfer of the data, it implies some
  form of modifications to the file.  If you require more than the
  transfer of the file, then far more details on the files and on
  the application(s) and such will be required.

:I do have a third system available that may assist in the transfer. 
:It is a VAX 7000 running OpenVMS V6.2 with a PC networked in using 
:Pathworks.

  In practice, use of PC systems can be good way to corrupt the 
  file(s) being transfered.  I would strongly discourage the novice 
  OpenVMS user from getting any PC storage involved in a file transfer.
  But your focus on the PC implies you are familiar with it, or that
  there are some (unspecified or specified) tools on the PC that you
  believe you need.

  As mentioned elsewhere, the most common approach for transfering 
  files between OpenVMS and UNIX -- Tru64 UNIX or otherwise -- is FTP.
  On OpenVMS, you will need to have a TCP/IP stack installed and 
  configured, such as TCP/IP Services.

  Alternatives to FTP include serving the file, using NFS.  OpenVMS 
  as well as most UNIX systems include both an NFS client and an NFS 
  server.


