In article <3CF4DAF9.36C3D76A@attbi.com>, Bob Willard <BobwBSGS@attbi.com> writes:

:Uh, right.  But many VAXen, like PCs, also have no boot code to directly
:boot from tape.  It was left up to the individual system design team
:to decide if there was a need for direct booting from tape, and many
:VAXen (and Alpen) did not support tape-boot.  IIRC, none of the 6xxx or
:7xxx or 8xxx or 9xxx VAXen could directly boot from tape.

  AFAIK, VAX 6000, VAX 7000, VAX 8000, VAX 9000, and VAX 10000 can all 
  boot from tape.  The DLT series cartridges were common console media 
  drives on some of these boxes, and a common distribution media, and 
  the VAX consoles can generally boot from any of various TMSCP tape
  devices. 

  Finding a VAX that cannot boot from CD-ROM is a whole easier than 
  finding a VAX that cannot boot from tape, and may be what you are
  recalling.  Alternatively, satellite bootstraps -- the upper-end
  VAX boxes could generally not bootstrap as cluster satellites.
  Or maybe you're thinking of booting the console off the console
  media, then booting the VAX off of the target device...  The VAX
  consoles -- for those VAX systems that had a dedicated console
  processor -- regularly booted from disk, but the console could then
  convince the VAX to boot off any supported installation device...
  And magtape was one of the standard installation media, either
  nine-track magtape or (for "newer") systems DLT...


